---
import { localeFromPath } from '@portfolio/i18n';
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ImageCompressor from '../../components/media/ImageCompressor';
---
<ToolLayout title="Image compressor" seoTitle="Free image compressor: JPEG, PNG, WebP, and AVIF" description="Compress, convert, crop, rotate, and resize images in your browser. Files never leave your device." category="Media" image="image" kind="MultimediaApplication" app>
  <ImageCompressor locale={localeFromPath(Astro.url.pathname)} client:load />
  <div slot="guide" class="flex flex-col gap-8">
    <div class="prose-copy"><h2>Smaller images, without uploading them.</h2><p>Add one or more images. Each one is rotated, cropped, resized, and re-encoded by your browser’s own image encoder as soon as it loads. Change a setting and every image updates. Nothing is sent to a server.</p></div>
    <div class="faq">
      <details><summary>Which format should I pick?</summary><div class="prose-copy"><p>WebP is the safe default: much smaller than JPEG and supported by every current browser. AVIF is smaller still, but only some browsers can write it, so it is disabled where unavailable. JPEG works everywhere but has no transparency; transparent areas become white. PNG is lossless and usually larger; use it for screenshots or graphics that need exact pixels.</p></div></details>
      <details><summary>What do quality and resize do?</summary><div class="prose-copy"><p>Quality controls how much detail the encoder may discard; 70–85% usually looks the same as the original. Resize scales the image down so its longest side is at most the chosen size. It never scales up. For large photos, resizing saves more than lowering quality.</p></div></details>
      <details><summary>How do crop and rotate work?</summary><div class="prose-copy"><p>Edits are applied in this order: rotate, crop, resize, encode. Choose a crop ratio, then drag the box or its corners on the preview. With the box focused, arrow keys move it and Shift + arrow keys resize it. Each image keeps its own edits.</p></div></details>
      <details><summary>What are the limits?</summary><div class="prose-copy"><p>Metadata such as camera details and location is removed, because the image is redrawn. Animated GIFs keep only their first frame. HEIC photos open only in browsers that can read them, such as Safari. Very large images can run out of memory on phones. A result can be larger than the original if the file was already well optimized.</p></div></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</ToolLayout>
